<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.PlanConfirmation"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False">

    <!--
        THE HEAVY ASK AT THE FOOT OF A PLAN SHEET: why it is heavy, and what it takes to go ahead -
        the entry's name typed back on one entry, or a refusal on a selection.

        ITS OWN FILE SINCE 2026-09-29, AND THE SIZE RATCHET POINTED AT IT - PlanFooter.xaml stood one
        line under the markup share that counts as "near", and the refusal of backlog 475 needed
        lines. The seam is the one GUI rule 9 names rather than a block of lines: this section has a
        state of its own (what was typed) and controls of its own, and the rest of the footer is
        the buttons and what stands in their way.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a UserControl is a ContentControl and arrives with
        both - `docs/10` trap 8, a silent extra Tab stop in front of the box.
    -->

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <BooleanToVisibilityConverter x:Key="WhenTrue" />
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <!--
        THE HEAVIEST CONFIRMATION THIS PROJECT HAS, AND IT IS ALMOST NEVER ON THE SCREEN.
        `docs/11` 9.2 point 2 puts the weight of an ask on the size of what it does, so this
        appears only where ending a process takes down more than the entry somebody named,
        or takes down one the machine will not run without. Measured 2026-08-01: 105 of 110
        processes on this machine host exactly one service, so the ordinary forcing plan
        shows the footer that shipped and nothing more.

        THE WHOLE BLOCK GOES WHEN IT IS NOT WANTED, heading included - backlog 203, which
        this window has already paid for once. A label reading "Type Spooler to confirm"
        over a plan that never asked for it would leave the button grey with nothing on
        screen explaining what would turn it on.
    -->
    <StackPanel x:Name="ConfirmSection"
                Visibility="{Binding AsksHeavily, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}">
        <!--
            WHY THE NAME HAS TO BE TYPED, OVER THE BOX IT HAS TO BE TYPED INTO, since
            2026-09-16. The sentence saying the machine would go down used to stand in
            "Worth knowing" - in the half of the sheet that scrolls, in plain white beside
            "it will be back after the next restart" - and the label under it said only
            "Type PlugPlay to confirm". The owner's words: the person does not know what
            they are confirming. The argument at the head of PlanFooter.xaml for putting the box
            in the footer - a confirmation somebody can skip past is not one - is the
            argument for its reason standing here too, and it leaves "Worth knowing" rather
            than being said twice.

            THE BLOCK IS THE REFUSED ONE, NOT A THIRD. Its red rule has meant "stop and read
            this" since 2026-09-02, and a block of its own in the same red would be a second
            name for one look. The text style is the one place this sheet weighs a sentence:
            DangerText, in Text.xaml, with the measurement beside it.
        -->
        <Border Style="{StaticResource PanelBlockRefused}"
                Visibility="{Binding HasDanger, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}">
            <TextBlock x:Name="PlanDanger"
                       AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planDanger"
                       Text="{Binding Danger}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Style="{StaticResource DangerText}" />
        </Border>

        <!--
            A SELECTION IS REFUSED RATHER THAN ASKED, backlog 475, the owner's decision of
            2026-09-29. The sentence above names the entries, and this one says what to do about
            them - in the place and the style of the label it stands in for, because it answers the
            same question: what does it take to go ahead from here.
        -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="PlanRefusesSelection"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planRefusesSelection"
                   Text="{DynamicResource gui.plan.confirm.selection}"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Visibility="{Binding RefusesSelection, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}"
                   Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

        <!--
            UpdateSourceTrigger IS THE WHOLE MECHANISM. A TextBox tells its binding on losing
            focus by default, so without this line the name would be typed, the button would
            stay grey, and it would come alive only when somebody clicked somewhere else -
            which reads exactly like a confirmation that does not work.
        -->
        <StackPanel x:Name="TypingPart"
                    Visibility="{Binding NeedsTyping, Converter={StaticResource WhenTrue}}">
            <TextBlock AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planConfirmLabel"
                       Text="{Binding TypeToConfirm}"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

            <TextBox x:Name="ConfirmBox"
                     AutomationProperties.AutomationId="planConfirm"
                     AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding TypeToConfirm}"
                     ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.plan.confirm.hint}"
                     Margin="{StaticResource MarginBelowSearch}"
                     Text="{Binding Typed, UpdateSourceTrigger=PropertyChanged}" />
        </StackPanel>
    </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
